# sql_review/: rules for {{ app_slug }}

SQL a person can open, run, and trace to every metric on every page of this app.
Data notes (grain, definitions, quirks, freshness) live in `../AGENTS.md`, not here.

## Layout

- `index.yaml`: the only file to edit by hand here. Pages, their metrics in on-screen order,
  the app query behind each metric, token samples, bind values, objects read, review window.
- `NN_<page>.sql`: generated, one per page; `NN` is the page's position in the app's navigation.
- `README.md`: the human index; the tables between its markers are generated.
- `app_specific_reporting_objects/<DATABASE>.<SCHEMA>.<OBJECT>.sql`: maintained DDL for objects
  built to make this app work, one file per object.

## Page file format

- Lines 1-8 are the header; the first metric tag is line 9 and its SQL starts on line 10.
- Each section starts with a tag line `--N_key` (N = on-screen position, key = the metric key in
  `index.yaml`), directly above its SQL, and ends with one `;`. One blank line between sections.
- Each section runs on its own: its `params` CTE holds the review window, `{TOKENS}` carry their
  sample values, and `:binds` read `params` (`params.start_date` in `index.yaml`).
- Reordering metrics or pages renumbers files and tags; the key stays the stable name.

## Editing rules

- Never edit a generated page file or the generated README tables: `check` reports it as a hand
  edit. Change `index.yaml` or `../queries/*.sql`, then run `streamsnow sql-review generate {{ app_slug }}`.
- Metric keys: snake_case, five words or fewer, describing the value shown (`revenue_by_region`).
- Every metric needs exactly one `review_value("<key>", value)` call on its page, wrapping the
  value its visual shows (`from review import review_value`). `check` matches them to the index.
- A query file that is only inlined into others is listed under `fragments:` with a reason.

## Comments in queries

- Every CTE gets a one-line comment directly above its name saying what it is for. For the first
  CTE, put `WITH` on its own line, then the comment, then `name AS (`.
- Every non-obvious filter, join, or CASE gets a one-line comment saying why.
- One line per comment, 100 characters or fewer. Never restate the SQL. Roughly one comment per 5
  to 10 SQL lines; comments under about a quarter of the query.
- `check` enforces the CTE comments and line length; the rest is the reviewer's judgement.

## Objects (app_specific_reporting_objects/)

- One file per object, headed by `-- Object:`, `-- Purpose:`, `-- Used by:` (generated from
  `index.yaml`), and `-- Grants:` (must match the object's `grants:` in `index.yaml`).
- List each object under `objects:` in `index.yaml` and name it in the `reads:` of every metric
  that reads it. `check` fails on a DDL file nothing reads, or an object with no DDL file.
- Objects in app data are applied by the deploy job (`streamsnow objects-sql`). A human applies
  DDL outside app data; Claude deploys it only when the user explicitly says to.
- These files are DDL on purpose: they are exempt from the read-only guard and no review command
  ever runs them.

## Commands

- `streamsnow sql-review generate {{ app_slug }}`: write page files, README tables, Used by lines.
- `streamsnow sql-review check {{ app_slug }}`: offline gate (pre-commit, CI, `validate-app`):
  index, provenance, markers, objects, sqlfluff lint of `../queries/*.sql`, comment rules.
- Lint settings are the repo's `.sqlfluff`.
- `/sql-review {{ app_slug }}` runs the live review against Snowflake: `streamsnow sql-review
  probe`, `run`, `bench`, `compare` (the screen against `run`, from review preview mode) and
  `log`. Their evidence stays local under `.streamsnow/sql-review/`.

## Review log (review_log/)

- `log` writes `review_log/YYYY-MM-DD_<shortsha>.md` and points the README's "Latest review" at it.
- Commit the log and the README together, alone: `chore(sql-review): {{ app_slug }} review <date>`.
  Fixes go in their own commits. Run `streamsnow sql-review check {{ app_slug }}` first; the
  pre-commit hook runs it too.
- The sign-off block is for a person. Claude never fills it in.
- Never record row-level data or small-group breakdowns in a log or a finding: only pass or fail,
  row counts and top-level totals.

